Write an Elixir function that, given a string (as a binary), returns the number of distinct non-empty subsequences of that string. The result should be computed modulo 10^9+7. A subsequence is a sequence derived from the string by deleting zero or more characters without changing the order of the remaining characters. Two subsequences are considered distinct if they are formed from different sets of indices, even if the resulting strings are the same. The function must handle strings up to 10^5 characters efficiently.

